Q2. Does a non-contiguous transposed view work? (Finding #17's premise)
============================================================================
  METHODOLOGY NOTE. The first version of this spike transposed on the host and
  then called .to(device), which lets the transfer materialize the result — so
  it proved nothing. The meaningful test moves the HF weight to the device
  first and transposes THERE, producing a genuinely non-contiguous device
  tensor. That is also the realistic case: in a kernelized model the weights
  already live on the device.

  C. transpose on host, then move (may materialize)
      H=1024 I=3072 B=1 S=128 BxS=128 dtype=float32 weights=host_view
      on-device is_contiguous: gate=True up=True down=True
      cos_sim=0.999989  max_diff=1.452e-02  wall=0.1s  PASS
  D. move to device, then transpose THERE  <-- the real test
      H=1024 I=3072 B=1 S=128 BxS=128 dtype=float32 weights=device_view
      on-device is_contiguous: gate=True up=True down=True
